%description:

Tcp.adaptiveDelayedAcks:linux-pingpong -- after a reply, the ACK of a request goes with the next reply.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "[...] and a pingpong mode, which delays the ACKs of interactive
traffic".

The classes: rfc (false), and three facets of true (LinuxTcp); this is the facet of the
pingpong mode. The code: data that host2 sends within one ATO of the last arrival is a reply
(dataSent()), and a reply ends the quickack mode (inQuickackMode()), so the ACK of the next
request waits, and the reply carries it. This test separates the facet from "rfc", in which
the first request gets no ACK at once, and from a quickack mode without the pingpong mode,
in which the second request gets an ACK at once too.

Host under test: host2, an echo server that replies 10 ms after each request. Peer: host1
sends a request of 100 bytes at 0.2 s and another at 0.3 s. Observation: level 1: host2
acknowledges the first request at once, without data; for the second request, host2 sends no
ACK without data, and its reply acknowledges the request.

%# parameter: Tcp.adaptiveDelayedAcks
%# class: linux-pingpong
%# value: true
%# key: *.host2.tcp.adaptiveDelayedAcks
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpAdaptiveDelayedAcksLinuxPingpong")
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                  .capture("isn", "tcp.sequenceNo")
                  .describe("the SYN of host1")
                  .within(0.5))
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.sequenceNo == {isn} + 1 && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
                  .describe("the first request at host2")
                  .within(0.5))
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.ackBit == true")
                  .first()
                  .assertPacket("pk.dataLength == tcp.headerLength && tcp.ackNo == {isn} + 101")
                  .describe("the ACK of the first request, at once and without data")
                  .within(0.001))
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.sequenceNo == {isn} + 101 && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
                  .describe("the second request at host2")
                  .within(0.2))
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.ackBit == true")
                  .first()
                  .assertPacket("pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength && tcp.ackNo == {isn} + 201")
                  .describe("the reply of host2, which acknowledges the second request")
                  .within(0.05));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpAdaptiveDelayedAcksLinuxPingpong compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host2.tcp.adaptiveDelayedAcks = true    # the parameter under test
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 0B
*.host1.app[0].sendScript = "0.2 100; 0.3 100"
*.host2.app[0].echoFactor = 1
*.host2.app[0].echoDelay = 10ms
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpAdaptiveDelayedAcksLinuxPingpong: PASS
